"How much salt is in one liter of seawater?": "1986e1272295",
"If you have ever swallowed a mouthful of seawater, you know how salty it is. But the rain that falls into the sea is not salty, and neither are the rivers that run into it. So how did the sea get its salt? The answer is a slow story, and it begins on land.": "0f5e57661277",
"If you have ever swallowed a mouthful of seawater, you know how salty it is. But the rain that falls into the sea is not salty, and neither are the rivers that run into it. So how did the sea get its salt? The answer is a slow story, and it begins on land. Rainwater can dissolve rock. It does this very, very slowly, but it never stops. As rain runs over the ground and soaks through it, it picks up tiny amounts of minerals. Streams carry these minerals to rivers, and rivers carry them to the sea. There is so little in a river that you cannot taste it at all. Now think about what happens next. The sun warms the sea, and water rises into the air as vapor. But only the water rises. The minerals cannot turn into vapor, so they stay in the sea. The vapor becomes cloud, the cloud becomes rain, and the rain washes a little more out of the rocks. The water goes around again and again. The salt makes the trip only once. This has been going on for an enormous length of time, and tiny amounts add up. Today, about 3.5% of the weight of seawater is salt. If you dried up one liter of seawater, about 35 grams of salt would be left. Most of it is the same kind of salt that we put on food. Is the sea still getting saltier? Scientists think that it is not. Salt leaves the sea too, very slowly. Some is used by living things, and some ends up buried in the seabed. For a very long time, about as much salt has left as has arrived, so the sea has stayed about the same. People have used this for a very long time. If the sun can lift water out of the sea and leave the salt behind, then we can let the sun make salt for us. Let seawater into a shallow pond, wait for the sun and the wind to dry it, and collect what is left. Taiwan made salt this way for more than three hundred years, on the sunny coast of the southwest and, for a time, at Lukang in Changhua.": "a8cec57cc1e3",
